        <div id="overnight-baked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Protein Overnight Oats and Baked Oats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Overnight oats remove the heat problem entirely, so any powder works. Mix 40 g of oats, one scoop of protein, about 180 to 240 ml of milk and a spoon of chia seeds or yogurt in a jar, stir well, and refrigerate overnight. Casein and blends give the thickest, creamiest result; whey isolate gives a looser texture; plant powders absorb the most liquid, so add extra milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix the powder into the milk before adding the oats, or shake everything in a sealed jar, to avoid dry pockets at the bottom. In the morning, stir and add fruit. Overnight oats keep for two to three days in the fridge, which makes them a good meal-prep option for busy weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Baked oats are closer to a cake. Blend oats into a flour with egg, banana, milk, baking powder and protein powder, then bake. Here, blends and casein again do best, because whey isolate tends to make baked goods dry and rubbery. Use protein for no more than about a third of the dry ingredients. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">best protein powder for baking</a> guide covers ratios and which powders hold up in the oven.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more option is protein mug oats: microwave oats with water, stir, rest, then add the protein slurry and a spoon of yogurt. It takes under five minutes and is the easiest way to make protein oatmeal at work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stirring powder into boiling oats</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey sets into rubbery lumps when it meets liquid near boiling. Take the pot off the heat, wait about a minute, and then stir in the powder. This single change fixes most protein oatmeal problems, whatever powder you use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding dry powder straight to the bowl</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dry powder forms pockets when it meets hot, sticky oats. Whisk each scoop with two or three tablespoons of milk into a smooth slurry first, then stir it through. It takes thirty seconds and makes the texture far smoother.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not adding extra liquid</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein powder absorbs liquid, and casein and plant proteins keep thickening as they sit. Cook the oats with about an extra quarter cup of liquid per scoop, and loosen with milk at the end if the bowl turns stodgy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using clear whey or fruity flavors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear whey isolates and fruity cereal flavors are designed for cold drinks, not hot breakfasts. They clump easily and taste odd in porridge. Stick with vanilla, chocolate, cinnamon, maple or unflavored.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reheating whey oats in the microwave</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Reheating protein oatmeal made with whey is what produces a rubbery, grainy bowl. If you meal prep, make overnight oats instead, or add the protein slurry only after reheating the plain oats.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

